                <h2>Case Opening: How a Top-Performing Creative Became an 'Ineffective Exposure Machine'</h2>
<p>Last month, a tool app's advertising team reached out for help. They had been running ads for a paid productivity application in the North American market for two consecutive weeks. The creative was averaging 500,000 daily impressions with a 3.5% CTR—above average for tool apps. But after two weeks, they had only generated 23 paying users at a $47 CPA. The team lead said, "This creative can run, but it can't sell."</p>
<p>So where did things go wrong?</p>
<p>In the review meeting, the team blamed the landing page, copy, and bidding. But I asked them to isolate the creative alone and re-examine it using the "can run vs. can sell" framework.</p>
<h2>Three Dimensions to Identify if a Creative 'Runs' or 'Sells'</h2>
<h3>Dimension 1: User Engagement Behavior</h3>
<p>A creative that "runs" attracts clicks that quickly leave. A creative that "sells" makes users pause and take action. Focus on two key indicators:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Page Scroll Depth</strong>: After landing, do users scroll to the core value proposition area? For tool products, users need to see feature demos or comparison charts before they're willing to pay.</li>
<li><strong>CTA Button Interactions</strong>: Do users hover over or click the purchase button multiple times, or return to modify options? This indicates genuine consideration.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Dimension 2: Where the Conversion Funnel Breaks</h3>
<p>Open Meta Ads Manager and examine the conversion path within the attribution window.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Creative State</th>
<th>Add-to-Cart Rate</th>
<th>Checkout Initiation Rate</th>
<th>Payment Success Rate</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Runs but doesn't sell</td>
<td>Normal or high</td>
<td>Drops sharply</td>
<td>Low</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Actually sells</td>
<td>Normal</td>
<td>Normal</td>
<td>Normal</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>If your creative shows "normal add-to-cart but checkout drop-off," the problem is likely a mismatch between what the creative promises and what the product delivers—users were "tricked" into clicking but found the reality different.</p>
<h3>Dimension 3: Value Proposition Clarity</h3>
<p>Take your creative and ask someone who hasn't seen the product page to describe it in one sentence: What does this product do? What problem does it solve? Why buy now?</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Can't explain clearly</strong> → Runs but doesn't sell. Users were drawn in by emotional hooks, but the value proposition is vague.</li>
<li><strong>Can explain clearly</strong> → Has selling potential. Now check the downstream experience.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Four-Step Action Plan: Evolving from 'Can Run' to 'Can Sell'</h2>
<p>Back to the tool app case. After review, we identified it as a classic "runs but doesn't sell" scenario:</p>
<p><strong>Problem Diagnosis</strong>: The creative led with broad claims like "AI-powered" and "300% efficiency boost," but the landing page hero showed interface screenshots. Users clicking through expected concrete proof of "efficiency gains" but found none. The clicks were impulsive; the purchase decision required rationality—a disconnect.</p>
<p><strong>Action 1: Revise the Creative Hook</strong><br />
Replace "300% efficiency boost" with a specific scenario: "Save 5 hours on formatting weekly." Concrete numbers let users calculate value.</p>
<p><strong>Action 2: Add Social Proof Above the Fold</strong><br />
Include user testimonials or download counts on the landing page hero: "120,000 users have upgraded." This lowers trust barriers.</p>
<p><strong>Action 3: Optimize CTA Guidance</strong><br />
Change the original CTA from "Download Premium Now" to "Start 7-Day Free Trial, Cancel Anytime." Lower the first-decision barrier for users.</p>
<p><strong>Action 4: Split Test New Creative Groups</strong><br />
Keep the original creatives running (to consume budget), while launching a new creative group to test the revised approach. After two weeks of comparison:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Original Creative Group</th>
<th>New Creative Group</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>CPM</td>
<td>$12</td>
<td>$14</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CTR</td>
<td>3.5%</td>
<td>2.8%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPA</td>
<td>$47</td>
<td>$18</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Paid Conversions</td>
<td>23</td>
<td>89</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The new creative had a slightly lower CTR but achieved nearly 4x the conversion rate, reducing overall CPA by 62%.</p>
<h2>Final Thoughts: Running Is Basic Competence, Selling Is the Goal</h2>
<p>Many advertisers default to "can run" as their creative selection criterion—sufficient impressions, acceptable CTR, therefore the creative passes. But for出海 teams, the ultimate goal is commercial: selling products, courses, memberships, or subscriptions.</p>
<p>During reviews, ask one more question: Were users attracted by the product's value, or "tricked" by the creative's format?</p>
<p>Clarifying this point ensures optimization efforts stay on track.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Yiyu Media建议</strong>: If you're running creatives across multiple accounts, classify them using both "reach rate" and "conversion rate" in a matrix: double-high creatives get scaled aggressively, double-low creatives get paused immediately, and "runs but doesn't sell" creatives need landing page alignment checks.</p>
